Subject:  Re: Software audit, privacy, etc.
Date: 21 Sep 1993 15:21:06 -0400
Message-ID: <27nk72$h05@eff.org>
steve@estel.uindy.edu (Steve Spicklemire) writes:

[...]
>I'd especially like to know if the University can avoid the liability
>of illegal software on a faculty member's machine, if all the faculty
>sign documents accepting responsibility for keeping their own HDs
>free of illegally obtained software, removing the need for an
>exhaustive (and expensive) software audit process.
[...]

The U. of Oregon seems to think that such a declaration is sufficient.
I find their opinion credible because they made it right after paying
the Software Publisher's Assocation (SPA) $130,000 to settle alledged
copyright infringement. Indeed, it might have been part of the
settlement.

Suspensionless searches violate academic freedom. In the case of state
schools in the U.S. they can violate the Constitution, too.

