>  What happens when the "harassee" gives an explanation the "harasser" doesn't
>  find plausible?
>
>I don't think that the 'harassee' is obligated to explain to the 'harasser'.
>There are situations where you can't explain why something is uncomfortable, or
>offensive.  I'm talking about more or less 'private' situations here.  I can
>request that you not treat *me* in certain ways, but I have no business trying
>to change your public or private behavior with other people.

Well, your last statement may be all the clarification I need, but I 
still worry about someone being able to tell me that I must cease some
behavior PURELY because they find it offensive or harassing.  The 
following fell under the category of public behavior, but I was still
forced to comply:

While I was a grad student at Ohio State University, I put the following
limmeric in my .sig file:

	Handsome woman, lovely bust,
	Fine young fellow: stirred up lust.
		Baby's diapers,
		Bottom wipers,
	Years of struggle, coffin, dust.

After some weeks, I was told that some women on the faculty or staff
(they were never identified to me) found the poem offensive and / or
harassing, and that I had to stop using it.  None of my queries about
why it was offensive or who found it so were answered.  

